; The default graphics set: 41 pictures and the objects' names. DEFAULT on the
; disc is this set as the Graphics Designer saves it, and IO, the mission,
; carries it too, as MISSION packs it; both are made from this one
; description (src/default.6502 and src/io.6502).
;
; Each picture is rows of pixels, two characters a pixel (a MODE 5 pixel is
; twice as wide as it is tall), "." for logical colour 0 and "1" to "3" the
; others. The Graphics Designer shows them black, blue, yellow and red; in
; the game each level's palette says. Large pictures are 16 x 32 pixels (4 x 4
; character cells), small ones 8 x 16; sprites.6502inc's mode5_pictures
; stores them a column of cells at a time, the way the designer and the game
; read them.
;
; The designer numbers the pictures as slots, &00-&28; the game uses them by
; slot too, so each slot has a part to play (as io.6502inc names them):
;
;   &00-&0F  large: a picture for each type of map cell
;   &10-&1F  small: the objects' icons
;   &20-&23  large: the player
;   &24-&27  large: second pictures for cell types 5, 7, 8 and A
;   &28      large: a cell marked as holding an object that isn't there
;
; and the names of icons 0-14. A graphics file is slots &00-&27 (&E00 bytes,
; default_graphics()) then MISSION's "names" (&134 bytes, default_names()): slot
; &28 and the object names. A mission keeps the names first and the graphics
; last.
;
; The map key characters in brackets are the ones level maps are drawn with
; (MAP_KEY in leveldata.6502inc).

INCLUDE "sprites.6502inc"

; Slots &00-&0F: a picture for each type of map cell, by type.
